<p>The UN General Assembly voted on Wednesday to designate the transatlantic slave trade and the enslavement of Africans as &quot;the gravest crime against humanity,&quot; adopting a Ghana-led resolution by 123 votes to three, with 52 abstentions. The measure was backed by the African Union and Caricom, and supporters said it marked a significant act of historical recognition and a step toward healing for communities still affected by slavery’s legacy. The three votes against came from the United States, Israel and Argentina, while Britain and European Union member states abstained. Beyond recognition, the resolution calls for dialogue on reparations and restorative justice, urges states involved in the slave trade to engage with affected communities, and supports the return of cultural items to their countries of origin without charge. UN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres said the trade &quot;struck at the core of personhood&quot; and that its defenders built racist ideology to justify it. Ghana’s President John Mahama, who attended the vote, said the resolution was meant to safeguard memory and pursue a route to healing and reparative justice, underscoring how this vote has turned long-standing demands from African and Caribbean states into a formal UN position at the highest multilateral level.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/eq3nXJkLp3GPteC_hT_ofgN2VDRhcHJxPA1IzZuhyIXRi1ZeER69SDsMILNi69u8oo3SLQUCJPXpy_tasd7VYBS3Pd-sQhU0E1WCcxyz_ercD07FzU5IxeUllU-3mCdxQXaGlJRe1kVK-PbGGiPJddyJ7szQjuixoS0E_znuqZRsqSrGyI_lOYEE37Txv5Z0I9-4ed9Dcbyo0IGNmBp_8BLiLzV2LeKr' alt='President John Mahama of Ghana during the U.N. General Assembly last year. Ghana introduced a U.N. resolution to recognize the trans-Atlantic slave trade as the “gravest crime against humanity” this week.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Legal wording: The resolution describes both the trafficking of enslaved Africans and the racialized chattel enslavement of Africans as the gravest crime against humanity, language supporters and legal experts said could strengthen future accountability claims.</li><li>Funding question: BBC reported that the resolution calls for an apology and contributions to a reparations fund, but does not specify an amount.</li><li>Coalition breadth: El País said the text had support from much of the Global South, showing the vote reached beyond African and Caribbean sponsors alone.</li></ul><blockquote>Today, we come together in solemn solidarity to affirm truth and pursue a route to healing and reparative justice. The adoption of this resolution serves as a safeguard against forgetting. - President John Mahama</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Ghanaian government: Officials described the initiative as a &quot;legal claim&quot; tied to accountability for abuses committed by colonizers, arguing the Assembly’s wording could matter in future redress efforts. (<a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-03-25/ghana-is-pursuing-a-un-resolution-that-considers-african-slavery-the-most-serious-crime-against-humanity.html'>El País</a>)</li><li>African Union and Caricom: The two blocs backed the resolution as a concrete step toward remedying historical wrongs and giving reparatory justice broader international backing. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/news/2026/mar/25/un-votes-slave-trade-gravest-crime-against-humanity-reparatory-justice'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>African Freedom Institute: Its president, Franklin Nyamsi, said recognition should lead to concrete action including reparations, educational reform and broader efforts to address continuing post-colonial dependencies. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/africa/636243-slavery-recognition-opens-accountability/'>RT</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/africa/article/3347954/un-designates-african-slave-trade-gravest-crime-against-humanity'>UN designates African slave trade as ‘gravest crime against humanity’</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a hr